<h2 id="sheet">The sheet and its frame</h2>
<p class="intro">The document itself: what a chart is printed on, and the marks
a maker leaves at its edges.</p>

<p class="term">Cartouche</p>
<p class="def">The decorative title panel on an antique-style chart.</p>

<p class="term">Colophon</p>
<p class="def">The maker's note at the foot of a book or sheet, saying who made
it and when. Vellum's journal signs off with one, and the seed it names is the
whole world in a single number.</p>

<p class="term">Docket</p>
<p class="def">The summary endorsement written on the outside of a folded
document, so it could be identified without unfolding. Vellum's verso carries a
docket line for the same reason.</p>


<p class="term">Neat line</p>
<p class="def">The ruled border enclosing a map's content, where the chart
formally ends and the margin begins.</p>

<p class="term">Plate</p>
<p class="def">A single printed sheet or figure, from the engraved copper plate
it was once pulled from. Vellum's rainfall and temperature sheets are plates in
this sense.</p>

<p class="term">Recto</p>
<p class="def">The front of a chart: the side the map is drawn on. Turn the
sheet over in the Explorer to find its verso.</p>

<p class="term">Surveyor's glass</p>
<p class="def">The hand lens kept on the chart table, and the Explorer's
magnifier named for it. Lean closer over an antique chart and the surveyor
redrafts the window under the glass as a finer regional survey, its coastline
inked in and its newly placed names drying by tier; stand back and the full
sheet returns.</p>

<p class="term">Verso</p>
<p class="def">The back of a chart. Real charts were folded into pilot books
and docketed on the reverse, so Vellum's verso carries a docket line, the
surveyor's attribution, and a survey-office stamp. Turn the sheet in the
Explorer to read it.</p>

<h2 id="chart">On the chart itself</h2>
<p class="intro">These print as literal legend or key text on a rendered chart.</p>

<p class="term">Contour line</p>
<p class="def">A line joining points of equal elevation. Its two cousins below,
the isohyet and the isotherm, do the same for rainfall and for temperature.</p>

<p class="term">Hypsometric tint</p>
<p class="def">Shading land by elevation band, the coloring scheme used on
the topographic style.</p>

<p class="term">Isohyet</p>
<p class="def">A line connecting points of equal rainfall, the contour lines
on the Rainfall plate.</p>

<p class="term">Isotherm</p>
<p class="def">A line connecting points of equal temperature, the contour
lines on the Temperature plate.</p>

<p class="term">League</p>
<p class="def">An old unit of distance, roughly three miles, being about as far
as a person walks in an hour. Vellum's scale bars are ruled in leagues, and its
daily hunt measures your guess in them.</p>

<p class="term">Realm seat</p>
<p class="def">A settlement that serves as a realm's seat of power: the
capital, or one of its smaller provincial seats.</p>

<p class="term">Rhumb line</p>
<p class="def">A straight line of constant compass bearing, radiating from
the compass rose in the old navigator's style.</p>


<h2 id="soundings">Soundings &amp; sea marks</h2>
<p class="intro">What the nautical style prints on the water.</p>

<p class="term">Fathom</p>
<p class="def">A unit of water depth equal to six feet (about 1.8 meters),
the traditional unit for nautical soundings. Vellum's depth readings are
labeled in fathoms.</p>

<p class="term">Ocean current</p>
<p class="def">A steady drift of water across the open sea, drawn as a curved
arrow. A ship that works with one arrives sooner than her log suggests.</p>

<p class="term">Prevailing wind</p>
<p class="def">A world's dominant wind direction, drawn as arrows on
nautical charts.</p>

<p class="term">Rock awash</p>
<p class="def">A nautical chart mark for a rock exposed or barely covered at
the surface, a hazard to ships.</p>

<p class="term">Shoal / danger line</p>
<p class="def">A tinted shallow-water zone on nautical charts, bounded by a
dashed line marking the hazardous edge.</p>

<p class="term">Sounding</p>
<p class="def">A numbered depth reading, in fathoms, scattered over open
water on nautical charts.</p>

<h2 id="sea">Coming in from the sea</h2>
<p class="intro">The voyage journal is written in a working sailor's language.
These are the words in it most likely to mislead, because each one has a
harmless everyday meaning as well.</p>

<p class="term">Bar</p>
<p class="def">A ridge of sand, shingle, or gravel thrown up across the mouth
of a river or harbor, shallow enough to stop a ship until the tide serves. The
same word names the gravel banks a riverman knows further upstream.</p>

<p class="term">Double</p>
<p class="def">To sail around a headland. To double a point closely is to cut
it fine, saving distance and risking the shore.</p>

<p class="term">Hand</p>
<p class="def">A scribe's individual handwriting, so a manuscript written in
"two hands" passed between two people. Also, at sea, to hand a sail is to take
it in and furl it.</p>

<p class="term">Holding ground</p>
<p class="def">The quality of the seabed for gripping an anchor. Good holding
ground is thick mud or stiff clay; a pilot who speaks ill of it means you may
drag in the night.</p>

<p class="term">Pilot</p>
<p class="def">A local expert taken aboard to steer a ship through water only
long practice can read. The pilot outranks the captain in his own approaches.</p>

<p class="term">Roads / roadstead</p>
<p class="def">A sheltered patch of open water off a coast where ships may lie
at anchor, short of a true harbor. When the journal says the survey made the
roads before dark, nobody has touched a road.</p>

<p class="term">Slack tide</p>
<p class="def">The still interval at the turn, when the tide is neither running
in nor out. The moment to move a ship in tight water.</p>

<p class="term">Warp</p>
<p class="def">To move a ship by hauling on a line made fast to a fixed point,
rather than under sail. The lines themselves are warps, and a crew warps in when
the wind will not serve.</p>

<h2 id="waterfront">The waterfront</h2>
<p class="intro">The built edge of a harbor town, as the gazetteer describes it.</p>

<p class="term">Breakwater</p>
<p class="def">A barrier of stone built out into the sea to take its force off
the water behind, so boats can lie quiet in weather.</p>

<p class="term">Chandler</p>
<p class="def">A shore merchant who outfits ships: rope, canvas, tar, lamp oil,
and salt provisions. A working port keeps a row of them along the front.</p>

<p class="term">Moorings</p>
<p class="def">The fixed places a ship ties up to in a harbor. Inner moorings
in a tidal port can be left dry twice a day.</p>

<p class="term">Quay</p>
<p class="def">A built stone landing running along the water, where ships lie
alongside to work cargo. Older charts spell it <em>key</em>, and pronounce it
that way still.</p>

<p class="term">Strand</p>
<p class="def">The shore itself, treated as a place rather than a line: the
beach a boat is run up on and a landing party crosses.</p>

<p class="term">Waterman</p>
<p class="def">A boatman who works a river for hire, ferrying goods and people.
A good one knows every gravel bar between his town and the sea.</p>

<p class="term">Wharf</p>
<p class="def">A landing structure ships lie against to load and unload. Where
a quay is the built bank itself, a wharf is the platform put out from it.</p>

<h2 id="river">The river &amp; the fen</h2>
<p class="intro">Inland water, and the words for working it.</p>


<p class="term">Beck</p>
<p class="def">A brook or small stream, a northern English word taken from Old
Norse and still living in place names across the Dales.</p>

<p class="term">Fen</p>
<p class="def">Low waterlogged ground, reed country, neither properly land nor
properly water. Fen-dwellers cut reed for thatch and worked at the water's
edge.</p>

<p class="term">Osier</p>
<p class="def">A willow shoot cut for basketry, grown by cropping the tree back
hard so it throws up long straight rods.</p>

<p class="term">Reach</p>
<p class="def">A straight stretch of river between two bends, long enough to be
named and fished as its own place. The daily hunt borrows the word for a
different job, meaning the eastern or western side of the chart, away from its
middle.</p>

<p class="term">Weir</p>
<p class="def">A low dam built across a river to raise its level or divert it,
often to drive a mill. A fish weir does the opposite work, funneling fish into
traps set in the gaps. In the oldest usage the word meant sea-weed.</p>

<h2 id="road">The road &amp; the market</h2>
<p class="intro">What the journal meets between one town and the next.</p>


<p class="term">Drover</p>
<p class="def">One who walks livestock to market on the hoof, sometimes for
weeks, along routes kept wide for the purpose.</p>

<p class="term">Fallow</p>
<p class="def">Ploughland deliberately left unsown for a season so it can
recover. Leave it longer in warm country and the forest takes it back.</p>

<p class="term">Market cross</p>
<p class="def">The stone cross set in a town's market place, marking its
chartered right to hold a market and serving as the spot where bargains were
struck and news was read out.</p>

<p class="term">May</p>
<p class="def">Hawthorn blossom, not the month. Hedges white with may put the
journal's date in late spring without naming it.</p>

<p class="term">Pack train</p>
<p class="def">A file of laden animals carrying goods where the way is too
steep or too rough for carts.</p>

<p class="term">Reeve</p>
<p class="def">A local official set over a village or manor, collecting dues and
answering for the place to its lord. A traveler with business asks for the
reeve.</p>

<p class="term">Threshing floor</p>
<p class="def">The hard swept ground, often shared by a whole village, where
grain is beaten from the straw after harvest.</p>

<h2 id="court">The court &amp; the realm</h2>
<p class="intro">The vocabulary of rule, as the gazetteer describes a capital.</p>

<p class="term">Charter</p>
<p class="def">A written grant of rights from a ruler, to a town, a guild, or a
market. Old charters outlive the hands that can still read them.</p>

<p class="term">Citadel</p>
<p class="def">The fortress commanding a city, held as its last defence and
often as a garrison against the city itself.</p>

<p class="term">Envoy</p>
<p class="def">A ruler's representative sent to another court to carry terms.
Waiting is a large part of the work.</p>

<p class="term">Fealty</p>
<p class="def">The loyalty a vassal swears to a lord, binding both: service
upward, protection downward.</p>

<p class="term">Guildhall</p>
<p class="def">The hall of a trade guild, its meeting place, its court, and
frequently the richest building in a town after the cathedral.</p>

<p class="term">Levy</p>
<p class="def">Troops or money raised as an obligation rather than hired. A
realm's strength on paper is usually its levy.</p>

<p class="term">Tithe</p>
<p class="def">A tenth part of produce or income rendered as a due. Paying it
late is an old way of saying a place is only half loyal.</p>

<p class="term">Vassal</p>
<p class="def">One who holds land from a lord in return for service, and who may
have vassals of his own beneath him.</p>

<h2 id="climate">Weather &amp; climate</h2>

<p class="term">Biome</p>
<p class="def">A category of plant and animal life shaped by climate.
Tundra, taiga, steppe, savanna, and forest all appear across Vellum's
worlds.</p>

<p class="term">Glass</p>
<p class="def">The barometer. When the journal says the glass stood fair it is
reading the weather, not looking through anything. Period sailors called any
instrument of glass by the name, the surveyor's glass above among them.</p>

<p class="term">Orographic</p>
<p class="def">Having to do with how mountains shape weather, especially by
wringing rain out of wind forced upward: orographic lift on the wet side,
a rain shadow on the dry one.</p>


<p class="term">Windward / leeward</p>
<p class="def">The side of high ground facing into the wind, which catches
the rain, versus the sheltered side, which stays dry.</p>

<h2 id="heraldry">Heraldry (coats of arms)</h2>

<p class="term">Blazon</p>
<p class="def">To formally describe or display a coat of arms.</p>

<p class="term">Charge</p>
<p class="def">A symbol or figure placed on a shield.</p>

<p class="term">Colours</p>
<p class="def">A ship's identifying flag, the one piece of heraldry meant to be
read at a distance. A watch that knows your colours has recognized you before
anyone has hailed.</p>

<p class="term">Ordinary</p>
<p class="def">A simple geometric band across a shield: a cross, chevron,
bend, fess, or pale.</p>

<p class="term">Rule of tincture</p>
<p class="def">The heraldic law that color can't sit on color, nor metal on
metal, so a shield always reads clearly at a glance. Every coat of arms
Vellum draws obeys it.</p>

<p class="term">Tincture</p>
<p class="def">The limited heraldic palette: two metals (gold, <em>or</em>;
silver, <em>argent</em>) and five colors (red, <em>gules</em>; blue,
<em>azure</em>; black, <em>sable</em>; green, <em>vert</em>; purple,
<em>purpure</em>).</p>

<h2 id="geography">Geography &amp; place</h2>

<p class="term">Archipelago</p>
<p class="def">A scattered group of islands, one of Vellum's four map
types.</p>

<p class="term">Atelier</p>
<p class="def">An artist's workshop. Vellum calls itself "an atelier of
imaginary cartography" in its own footer.</p>

<p class="term">Confluence</p>
<p class="def">The point where two rivers meet.</p>

<p class="term">Estuary</p>
<p class="def">The widening mouth of a river where it meets the sea.</p>

<p class="term">Gazetteer</p>
<p class="def">A directory of place names. Vellum's gazetteer lists every
town on the map, grouped by realm, with a line or two of travelers'
notes.</p>

<p class="term">Hinterland</p>
<p class="def">The inland territory a city depends on, especially a
city-state's supporting countryside.</p>

<p class="term">Vellum</p>
<p class="def">Prepared animal skin used as a writing and drawing surface
for real medieval maps and manuscripts, the material this whole project
takes its name from.</p>

<p class="term">Watershed</p>
<p class="def">The boundary between two river drainage systems. Vellum's
realm borders prefer to follow these.</p>

<h2 id="airs">What a place smells of</h2>
<p class="intro">The gazetteer gives each town an air, drawn from what its
culture burns, brews, and grows. Several are worth knowing on their own.</p>

<p class="term">Attar</p>
<p class="def">Distilled flower oil, the costliest being rose. It takes a
great many petals to make a very little of it.</p>

<p class="term">Camphor</p>
<p class="def">A white aromatic crystal from an East Asian laurel, burned,
carried against sickness, and worked into chests to keep moths out.</p>

<p class="term">Copal</p>
<p class="def">A pale tree resin burned as incense across Mesoamerica, where
its smoke carried offerings upward.</p>

<p class="term">Copra</p>
<p class="def">Dried coconut kernel, pressed for its oil. The stuff of island
trade, and the smell of the sheds it is dried in.</p>

<p class="term">Frangipani</p>
<p class="def">A heavily scented tropical blossom that keeps its perfume long
after it has been picked.</p>

<p class="term">Kvass</p>
<p class="def">A mildly fermented drink made from rye bread, sour, cheap, and
everywhere in the northern towns.</p>

<p class="term">Sandalwood</p>
<p class="def">A dense aromatic heartwood, burned as incense and carved into
boxes that hold the scent for years.</p>

<h2 id="cargoes">What a place ships</h2>
<p class="intro">A town's cargoes come from the same table, and say as much about a
town as its walls do.</p>

<p class="term">Cochineal</p>
<p class="def">A brilliant crimson dye made from a dried scale insect farmed on
cactus. For centuries it was among the most valuable cargoes crossing an
ocean.</p>

<p class="term">Indigo</p>
<p class="def">The deep blue plant dye, difficult to make and long a luxury,
which is why blue cloth once signalled money.</p>

<p class="term">Iron bloom</p>
<p class="def">The spongy mass of iron and slag drawn from a smelting furnace,
sold on to be forged. Not yet a tool, but no longer ore.</p>

<p class="term">Lacquerware</p>
<p class="def">Wood built up with many thin coats of tree sap and polished
hard, producing a surface that is waterproof and nearly indestructible.</p>

<p class="term">Obsidian</p>
<p class="def">Volcanic glass, flaked to an edge finer than any metal blade,
and traded far from the few mountains that produce it.</p>

<p class="term">Quetzal feathers</p>
<p class="def">The iridescent green tail plumes of the quetzal, reserved for
rulers in Mesoamerica and taken from living birds, which were then released.</p>

<p class="term">Sable</p>
<p class="def">A dark, exceptionally soft northern fur, and a currency of the
taiga in its own right. Not to be confused with the heraldic <em>sable</em>
above, which simply means black.</p>

<h2 id="names">Words on your own map</h2>
<p class="intro">Each of Vellum's ten invented cultures borrows its realm
and place-name vocabulary from a real language or region that matches its
sound. Here's what those words mean where they come from, grouped by the
culture that uses them.</p>

<h3>Norden, the hard northern tongue</h3>

<p class="term">Fell</p>
<p class="def">A mountain or stretch of high, barren moorland, a word used
in Northern England and Scandinavia.</p>

<p class="term">Jarldom</p>
<p class="def">The territory or rank of a jarl, an Old Norse nobleman
roughly equal to an earl.</p>

<p class="term">March</p>
<p class="def">A borderland or frontier territory, the root of "the Welsh
Marches" and "margrave."</p>

<h3>Ordai, the steppe tongue</h3>

<p class="term">Bulak</p>
<p class="def">A spring or water source, a Turkic word that ends dozens of real
Central Asian place names. On open grassland, a reliable spring is where a town
begins.</p>

<p class="term">Dag / Uul</p>
<p class="def">Mountain: <em>dag</em> in Turkish, <em>uul</em> in Mongolian.
Both end real peak names across the steppe and the ranges south of it.</p>

<p class="term">Dalai</p>
<p class="def">Sea or ocean in Mongolian, the word behind "Dalai Lama," an
ocean of wisdom. On a landlocked steppe it names the great inland waters.</p>

<p class="term">Gol</p>
<p class="def">River, in Mongolian.</p>

<p class="term">Khanate</p>
<p class="def">The territory ruled by a khan.</p>

<p class="term">Khot / balik</p>
<p class="def">City, in Mongolian and in Old Turkic. Hohhot is the "blue city";
Beshbalik was the "five cities."</p>

<p class="term">Kol / Nor</p>
<p class="def">Lake, in Turkic and in Mongolian. Kyrgyzstan's Issyk-Kul and the
salt flat of Lop Nor both carry the word.</p>

<p class="term">Kurgan</p>
<p class="def">A burial mound raised over a grave and heaped with earth, left
in their thousands across the steppe by peoples who built little else that
lasted.</p>

<p class="term">Orda / horde</p>
<p class="def">A khan's camp and court, the moving capital of a steppe empire.
English borrowed the word as "horde" and lost the palace in it.</p>

<h3>Oromi, the island tongue</h3>

<p class="term">Atoll</p>
<p class="def">A ring-shaped coral reef, island, or islet chain enclosing a
lagoon.</p>

<p class="term">Chiefdom</p>
<p class="def">A hierarchical society under a paramount chief, more
organized than a tribe, less centralized than a state. Historically the
term used for pre-contact Hawaiian and other Pacific island societies.</p>

<h3>Sylvan, the woodland tongue</h3>

<p class="term">Downs</p>
<p class="def">Open, grassy chalk upland, like England's South Downs.</p>

<p class="term">Mere</p>
<p class="def">An old word for a lake or shallow pond.</p>

<p class="term">Tor</p>
<p class="def">A rocky hilltop or craggy outcrop.</p>

<p class="term">Vale</p>
<p class="def">An archaic word for valley.</p>

<p class="term">Wold</p>
<p class="def">Open, rolling, treeless upland, like England's Yorkshire
Wolds or the Cotswolds.</p>

<h3>Tezcal, the jungle tongue</h3>

<p class="term">Apan</p>
<p class="def">"On the water" in Nahuatl, from <em>atl</em>, water. It ends real
Mexican river names like the Papaloapan, and ends Vellum's Tezcal rivers the
same way.</p>

<p class="term">Cenote</p>
<p class="def">A limestone sinkhole opening onto the groundwater beneath, the
Yucatan's natural well and its only water on a peninsula with almost no surface
rivers. From Yucatec Maya <em>ts'onot</em>.</p>

<p class="term">Fifth Sun</p>
<p class="def">In Mexica cosmology, the present age of the world: the fifth
creation, after four earlier suns each ended in catastrophe. Vellum's Tezcal
realms are dated by it.</p>

<p class="term">Tepetl</p>
<p class="def">Hill or mountain in Nahuatl, worn down to the <em>-pec</em> that
ends Chapultepec, "on grasshopper hill."</p>

<p class="term">-tlan</p>
<p class="def">"Place of," the commonest Nahuatl place ending: Mazatlan is the
place of deer. Its neighbor <em>-pan</em> means "on" or "over."</p>

<h3>Thalassic &amp; Draket, the seafaring and mountain-clan tongues</h3>

<p class="term">Dominion</p>
<p class="def">A territory under a ruler's control. Historically also the
term for self-governing territories of the British Empire.</p>

<h3>Tsuren, the tongue of the eastern isles</h3>

<p class="term">-gawa / -kawa</p>
<p class="def">River, in Japanese. <em>Kawa</em> softens to <em>gawa</em> when
it ends a compound, which is why the Edo river is the Edogawa.</p>

<p class="term">Mandate</p>
<p class="def">The Mandate of Heaven, the classical Chinese doctrine that a
ruler governs by heaven's warrant and forfeits it by misrule. A dynasty that
falls is held to have lost the mandate, not merely the war.</p>

<p class="term">-mori</p>
<p class="def">A forest or grove. Aomori, the city at the top of Honshu, is
the "blue forest."</p>

<p class="term">-shima</p>
<p class="def">Island. It voices to <em>-jima</em> in many names, Iwo Jima
among them, but not in all: Hiroshima keeps its <em>s</em>.</p>

<p class="term">Shogunate</p>
<p class="def">The rule of a shogun, the military governor who held the real
power in Japan for centuries while the emperor reigned in name.</p>

<p class="term">-yama</p>
<p class="def">Mountain, the <em>yama</em> that ends Okayama and Wakayama.</p>

<h3>Veshari, the desert tongue</h3>

<p class="term">Sultanate</p>
<p class="def">The territory or reign of a sultan, a Muslim sovereign.</p>

<p class="term">Wadi</p>
<p class="def">A valley or channel that runs dry except in the rainy
season, in North Africa and the Middle East.</p>

<h3>Zoryan, the taiga tongue</h3>

<p class="term">Balka</p>
<p class="def">A broad, grassed-over gully cut into the steppe by meltwater, an
old ravine gone soft at the edges.</p>

<p class="term">-grad</p>
<p class="def">Town, and originally a fortified one: Belgrade is the white
town, Novgorod the new one.</p>

<p class="term">Liman</p>
<p class="def">A shallow lagoon where a river mouth has been dammed off from
the sea by a sandbar, the classic Black Sea coastal form. The word reached
Slavic from Greek <em>limen</em>, a harbor.</p>

<p class="term">-ov</p>
<p class="def">A possessive ending: the place belonging to someone, as in
Rostov or Kirov. Its siblings <em>-sk</em>, <em>-in</em>, and <em>-itsa</em>
end town names the same way.</p>

<p class="term">Ozero</p>
<p class="def">Lake, in Russian.</p>

<p class="term">Planina</p>
<p class="def">A mountain range, in the South Slavic languages: the Balkans'
own Stara Planina is the "old mountain."</p>

<p class="term">Reka</p>
<p class="def">River.</p>

<p class="term">Taiga</p>
<p class="def">The word itself: English took it from Russian, which took it in
turn from a Turkic or Mongolic source in Siberia. What it names, the northern
conifer belt, is one of the biomes above.</p>

<p class="term">Tsardom</p>
<p class="def">The realm of a tsar, a title descended, by a long road, from
Caesar.</p>

<p class="term">Voivodeship</p>
<p class="def">The province of a voivode, a Slavic warlord turned provincial
governor. Poland still calls its provinces voivodeships.</p>
